cies with attributes."\n      },\n      "risk_perception_distribution": {\n        "value": "Beta(a,b) fitted to risk_perception",\n        "source": "agent_attributes.csv (risk_perception)",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Use method-of-moments or MLE; store (a,b) for sampling individual thresholds."\n      },\n      "adoption_model_form": {\n        "value": "logistic: P(mask_t=1) = \\u03c3(\\u03b1 + \\u03c1\\u00b7mask_{t-1} + \\u03b2_info\\u00b7info_t + \\u03a3_l \\u03b2_l\\u00b7frac_masked_neighbors_l + \\u03b3\'X_i)",\n        "source": "time-series CSV (transitions), adjacency (peer fractions), agent_attributes (X_i)",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "\\u03c1 captures persistence; X_i can include risk_perception and demographics; when peer fractions not observed, estimate \\u03b1, \\u03c1, \\u03b2_info first and calibrate \\u03b2_l via simulation."\n      },\n      "persistence_parameter": {\n        "value": "\\u03c1 estimated from logistic transition model",\n        "source": "time-series CSV",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Expect \\u03c1 > 0 and sizable; governs stickiness once adopted."\n      },\n      "abandonment_probability": {\n        "value": "P(mask_t=0 | mask_{t-1}=1) estimated as 1 - P(continue wearing)",\n        "source": "time-series CSV",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Estimate overall and stratified by demographics for heterogeneity."\n      },\n      "peer_influence_weights": {\n        "value": {\n          "family": "to be calibrated",\n          "work_school": "to be calibrated",\n          "community": "to be calibrated"\n        },\n        "source": "calibrated using simulated method of moments against time-series prevalence curves",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Initialize prior weights e.g., family>work>community (e.g., 0.5,0.3,0.2) then refine to match observed adoption dynamics."\n      },\n      "information_effect": {\n        "value": "\\u03b2_info (log-odds) from logistic model with received_info",\n        "source": "time-series CSV",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Consider both contemporaneous and 1-day lag terms; choose based on AIC/BIC."\n      },\n      "individual_susceptibility_mapping": {\n        "value": "\\u03b8_i = f(risk_perception_i) with f decreasing threshold or positive logit coefficient on risk_perception",\n        "source": "agent_attributes.csv (risk_perception), time-series CSV",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Option 1: threshold models where adoption if weighted neighbor fraction > \\u03b8_i = 1 - risk_perception_i; Option 2: logistic coefficient \\u03b2_risk."\n      }\n    },\n    "interaction_schedule": {\n      "daily_contacts_per_layer": {\n        "value": "c_l(i) = min(cmax_l, k_l(i) * s_l) where k_l is degree in layer l",\n        "source": "agent_attributes.csv (degrees) or adjacency degrees",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Choose scale s_l to reflect frequency (family highest). Calibrate s_l to match plausible daily contact counts if available."\n      },\n      "layer_influence_weights": {\n        "value": {\n          "family": 0.5,\n          "work_school": 0.3,\n          "community": 0.2\n        },\n        "source": "theory-driven prior; refined via calibration to match adoption trajectory",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Re-weight during calibration; ensure sum to 1 after adjusting for edge overlap."\n      },\n      "update_scheme": {\n        "value": "synchronous daily updates with exposure computed from previous day",\n        "source": "time-series daily granularity",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Avoids simultaneity bias; can also test asynchronous stochastic updates in sensitivity analysis."\n      }\n    },\n    "information_process": {\n      "info_arrival_rate": {\n        "value": "\\u03bb_info = mean(received_info) per agent-day",\n        "source": "time-series CSV (received_info)",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Use as baseline Poisson/Bernoulli rate for exogenous info events; allow targeted rates by occupation."\n      },\n      "campaign_targeting": {\n        "value": "optional: elevate info rates for selected demographics/occupations",\n        "source": "policy scenario design; informed by occupation distribution",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Use to test intervention strategies; not identifiable from data without additional context."\n      }\n    },\n    "observation_and_noise": {\n      "measurement_error_mask_state": {\n        "value": "assume zero or small false positive/negative rates; sensitivity analysis only",\n        "source": "no direct measurement data",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "If external validation exists, incorporate; otherwise keep at 0 and vary in robustness checks."\n      },\n      "missing_data_handling": {\n        "value": "forward-fill within spells; drop isolated missing days; verify one row per (agent,day)",\n        "source": "time-series CSV quality checks",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Ensure consistent transition estimation; document imputation rate."\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  "calibration_strategy": {\n    "preprocessing_steps": [\n      {\n        "step": "Validate and clean agent_attributes.csv",\n        "purpose": "Ensure total_connections equals sum of layer degrees; check ranges (risk_perception in [0,1]); parse booleans; handle missing values."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Construct or ingest multiplex network",\n        "purpose": "If adjacency JSON exists, load and compute per-layer degrees, reciprocity, overlap, clustering. If not, generate per-layer graphs via configuration models using degree targets and homophily constraints."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Harmonize IDs and merge datasets",\n        "purpose": "Join time-series and attributes by agent_id; drop agents without overlap or create placeholders."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Time-series integrity checks",\n        "purpose": "Verify one row per (agent, day); handle missing days; compute spells, daily transitions, and lagged variables (mask_{t-1}, received_info_{t}, received_info_{t-1})."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Compute peer exposure series (if network provided)",\n        "purpose": "For each agent-day, compute fraction of masked neighbors per layer from previous day to use in regression/calibration."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Stratification variables",\n        "purpose": "Create categorical dummies for age_group and occupation; compute degrees per layer for use as controls."\n      }\n    ],\n    "calibration_approach": "Use a two-stage strategy. Stage 1: Estimate individual-level transition dynamics without peer terms using the panel: logistic regression or mixed effects model of wearing_mask_t on wearing_mask_{t-1}, received_info_t (and lag), risk_perception, demographics, and optionally agent random intercepts. Extract baseline intercept, persistence (\\u03c1), information effect (\\u03b2_info), and risk coefficient (\\u03b2_risk). Stage 2: Calibrate peer influence and layer weights using simulation-based methods. If adjacency is available, augment the regression with lagged peer exposure fractions per layer to estimate \\u03b2_family, \\u03b2_work, \\u03b2_community directly. If not available, perform simulated method of moments or approximate Bayesian computation: generate networks consistent with degree distributions and homophily; simulate adoption with candidate \\u03b2_l and layer weights; minimize distance between observed and simulated moments (time path of prevalence, adoption and abandonment hazards, spell-length distribution, and cross-sectional variance by occupation/age). Iterate until convergence. Fit degree distribution models (Poisson/NegBin) and Beta distribution for risk_perception for input sampling. Use cross-validation on days to guard against overfitting.",\n    "validation_strategy": "Hold out a block of days (e.g., last 20%) and/or a subset of agents for out-of-sample validation. Compare simulated vs observed: (1) aggregate prevalence over time, (2) daily adoption and abandonment rates, (3) distribution of wearing spell lengths, (4) stratified prevalence by age_group and occupation, (5) if network available, spatial clustering of behavior (e.g., Moran\\u2019s I or assortativity of mask state). Perform posterior predictive checks and sensitivity analysis on layer weights and network symmetrization. Report calibration error metrics (RMSE of prevalence curve, KL divergence of spell-length distribution).",\n    "key_variables_to_calibrate": [\n      "\\u03b2_family, \\u03b2_work_school, \\u03b2_community (peer influence coefficients)",\n      "layer_influence_weights (family/work_school/community)",\n      "\\u03c1 (persistence) and abandonment probability",\n      "\\u03b2_info (information effect) and lag structure",\n      "risk_perception mapping (\\u03b2_risk or threshold function parameters)",\n      "daily_contacts_per_layer scaling factors",\n      "homophily mixing strengths and network symmetrization choice"\n    ]\n  },\n  "file_summaries": [\n    "Semantic metadata summary for agent_attributes.csv\\n\\n- Overall data structure and type\\n  - Flat CSV; one row per agent (unique agent_id).\\n  - Columns include integers, floats, booleans, and categorical strings.\\n  - No explicit network edges; only per-layer degree counts.\\n\\n- Meaning of keys/columns\\n  - agent_id (int): Unique identifier for each agent.\\n  - age (int): Agent\\u2019s age in years.\\n  - age_group (string): Categorical bin of age (e.g., Youth, Young Adult, Middle Age); redundant with age.\\n  - occupation (string): Socioeconomic/role category (e.g., Student, Blue Collar, White Collar); useful for homophily and layer assignment.\\n  - risk_perception (float 0\\u20131): Propensity to adopt mask-wearing given perceived risk; higher implies easier adoption.\\n  - initial_mask_wearing (bool): Initial behavioral state at t0.\\n  - family_connections (int): Target degree in the family/household layer.\\n  - work_school_connections (int): Target degree in the work/school layer.\\n  - community_connections (int): Target degree in the community/social layer.\\n  - total_connections (int): Redundant; equals family + work_school + community.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements\\n  - Multi-layer network semantics: the three connection columns indicate degrees per layer; total_connections is the sum across layers.\\n  - age_group is derived from age; may guide layer participation (e.g., students with higher school ties).\\n  - No nested records; relationships are implied, not enumerated.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\\n  - Agent construction: instantiate agents with attributes (id, age/age_group, occupation, risk_perception, initial_mask_wearing).\\n  - Network generation: build a three-layer graph using the per-layer degree targets (e.g., configuration model per layer with homophily by age/occupation). Ensure degree feasibility and reciprocity; total_connections is a validation check.\\n  - Interaction dynamics:\\n    - Influence weights by layer (e.g., family > work/school > community) and contact frequency derived from layer degrees.\\n    - Adoption rule: probability/threshold for switching to mask-wearing as a function of risk_perception, fraction of masked neighbors per layer, and layer weights. initial_mask_wearing seeds the diffusion.\\n    - Heterogeneity by demographics/occupation can modulate susceptibility, influence, or contact rates.\\n  - Validation/consistency: verify total_connections equals the sum of layer degrees; handle agents with zero degree in specific layers; note that age_group/occupation may contain edge cases and should not be assumed to be strictly realistic.",\n    "- Overall data structure and type:\\n  - JSON object representing a social network as adjacency lists.\\n  - Top-level keys are agent identifiers (as strings), each mapping to an object of contact lists.\\n- Meaning of keys or columns:\\n  - family: list of agent IDs with familial ties to the focal agent.\\n  - work_school: list of agent IDs representing workplace/school contacts.\\n  - community: list of agent IDs for broader community ties (neighbors, social groups, etc.).\\n  - all: deduplicated union of family, work_school, and community contacts for that agent.\\n- Relationships or nested elements:\\n  - Each agent node contains three layer-specific neighbor lists plus a flattened \\u201call\\u201d list.\\n  - Values are integer agent IDs; no explicit edge weights or directions are provided.\\n  - Reciprocity is not guaranteed by the data (an agent may list another who does not list them back); treat as directed unless you explicitly symmetrize.\\n  - Potential overlap across layers is possible; \\u201call\\u201d consolidates them.\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\\n  - Define one agent per top-level key (ID space appears to be roughly 0\\u2013999).\\n  - Build a multiplex network with three layers (family, work/school, community); use \\u201call\\u201d for fast neighbor iteration but retain layer labels for dynamics.\\n  - Assign layer-specific influence/contact parameters (e.g., family: strongest/highest frequency, work_school: medium, community: weakest/lowest frequency).\\n  - Schedule interactions by sampling neighbors per layer each timestep; compute mask adoption updates based on neighbors\\u2019 mask status with layer-weighted influence.\\n  - If modeling undirected influence, consider symmetrizing edges; otherwise, keep as directed for asymmetric exposure.\\n  - No agent attributes are included; initialize mask-wearing states, susceptibility, and compliance parameters externally.",\n    "- Overall data structure and type\\n  - CSV file in long/record format; each row is a time-stamped observation for one agent.\\n  - Granularity: one row per (agent_id, day). Primary key should be the tuple (agent_id, day).\\n  - Data types: day (int, discrete timestep), agent_id (int), wearing_mask (boolean), received_info (boolean).\\n\\n- Meaning of keys or columns\\n  - day: Discrete simulation timestep (e.g., t = 0, 1, 2, \\u2026).\\n  - agent_id: Unique identifier for an agent (node) in the social network.\\n  - wearing_mask: Agent\\u2019s behavioral state at that day (1 = wearing a mask, 0 = not wearing).\\n  - received_info: Whether the agent was exposed to an information event that day (e.g., message/campaign/peer message); likely a per-day flag, not cumulative.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements\\n  - Flat (no nested structures). Time series relationships are implied by grouping rows by agent_id and ordering by day.\\n  - No explicit network structure or neighbor relations in this file; social ties must come from another source or be synthetically generated.\\n  - Implicit temporal dependency: wearing_mask(t) depends on past states and possibly on received_info(t); may also depend on peers (not represented here).\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\\n  - Agent initialization: Use day 0 records to set initial mask-wearing state per agent.\\n  - Transition modeling: Estimate adoption and abandonment probabilities P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}, received_info_t, agent) from the panel data (e.g., Markov/logistic models). Consider agent-level heterogeneity if enough history per agent exists.\\n  - Information effects: Quantify the causal/associative impact of received_info on same-day or next-day adoption, informing rules for exogenous information shocks or campaign scheduling.\\n  - Persistence/inertia: Infer duration dependence (e.g., probability to continue wearing once adopted) from consecutive days.\\n  - Missing components: Since network edges and peer exposure are not included, the simulation should:\\n    - Ingest or generate a network (adjacency) separately.\\n    - Define peer influence rules using the simulated neighbor mask rates, combining them with the calibrated individual and information-driven transition probabilities.\\n  - Data hygiene assumptions: Ensure one unique row per (agent_id, day); handle boolean parsing (true/false); check for missing days per agent and decide on imputation or skipping in model fitting."\n  ]\n}\n\nPlease design a simulation model that addresses the task requirements. Your response should include:\n\n1. The most appropriate modeling approach (agent-based, system dynamics, network-based, etc.)\n2. The structure of the simulation (entities, behaviors, interactions)\n3. The environment representation (grid, continuous space, network, etc.)\n4. Necessary parameters and configuration options\n5. Initialization procedures\n6. The algorithms to implement key behaviors and interactions\n7. A plan for how the code should be structured\n8. Any prediction or evaluation period specified by the task (e.g., days 30-39), and include it as a `prediction_period` field in the model plan.\n9. The evaluation metrics required by the task, and include them as an `evaluation_metrics` list in the model plan.\n\nResponse Format:\nPlease structure your response as a JSON object with the following structure:\n{\n  "model_type": "agent_based | system_dynamics | network | etc.",\n  "description": "Brief description of the model approach",\n  "entities": [\n    {\n      "name": "Entity name",\n      "attributes": ["attribute1", "attribute2", ...],\n      "behaviors": ["behavior1", "behavior2", ...],\n      "initialization": {\n        "method": "random | data_driven | specified",\n        "parameters": { ... }\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  "behaviors": [\n    {\n      "name": "behavior_name",\n      "description": "Description of the behavior",\n      "applicable_to": ["Entity1", "Entity2", ...],\n      "parameters": { ... },\n      "algorithm": "Description of algorithm or approach"\n    }\n  ],\n  "interactions": [\n    {\n      "name": "interaction_name",\n      "description": "Description of the interaction",\n      "entities_involved": ["Entity1", "Entity2", ...],\n      "trigger": "proximity | time | state | etc.",\n      "effect": "Description of the effect"\n    }\n  ],\n  "environment": {\n    "type": "grid | continuous | network | etc.",\n    "dimensions": [x, y] or null,\n    "time_step": value,\n    "time_unit": "seconds | minutes | hours | days | etc."\n  },\n  "parameters": {\n    "param1": value1,\n    "param2": value2,\n    ...\n  },\n  "initialization": {\n    "population_size": number,\n    "random_seed": number,\n    ...\n  },\n  "algorithms": {\n    "algorithm1": "Description or name of algorithm",\n    "algorithm2": "Description or name of algorithm",\n    ...\n  },\n  "data_sources": [\n    {\n      "name": "data_source_name",\n      "purpose": "Purpose of using this data",\n      "fields_used": ["field1", "field2", ...]\n    }\n  ],\n  "code_structure": {\n    "files": [\n      {\n        "name": "filename.py",\n        "description": "Purpose of this file"\n      }\n    ],\n    "dependencies": [\n      "library1",\n      "library2",\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  "prediction_period": { "start_day": <integer>, "end_day": <integer> },\n  "evaluation_metrics": [ "metric1", "metric2", ... ]\n} '}]}], 'max_output_tokens': 10000, 'model': 'gpt-5-2025-08-07'}}
